body=f'''<!doctype html><html lang="en"><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>Research feasibility brief</title>
<style>body{{font:17px/1.65 system-ui,sans-serif;color:#21304b;background:#f4f7fb;margin:0}}main{{max-width:1060px;margin:auto;padding:48px 32px;background:white}}h1{{font-size:36px;line-height:1.18;max-width:850px}}h2{{font-size:25px;margin-top:44px}}h3{{font-size:19px}}.eyebrow,small{{color:#5e6d83}}.lead{{font-size:20px}}.callout{{padding:18px 22px;background:#eaf2ff;border-left:4px solid #355ec9}}.note{{background:#fff6e4;border-left-color:#c08a27}}table{{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:15px}}th,td{{padding:12px;border-bottom:1px solid #dce3ec;text-align:left;vertical-align:top}}th{{background:#eef3f9}}a{{color:#2555b5}}img{{width:100%;height:auto}}code{{background:#eef3f9;padding:2px 5px}}.flow{{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr);gap:12px}}.flow>div{{background:#edf3fb;border-radius:8px;padding:16px}}.abstract{{border:1px solid #dce3ec;padding:24px}}@media(max-width:650px){{main{{padding:24px 16px}}.flow{{grid-template-columns:1fr}}table{{font-size:12px}}th,td{{padding:7px}}}}@media print{{body{{background:white}}main{{padding:0}}h2{{break-after:avoid}}tr,img,.abstract{{break-inside:avoid}}}}</style>
<main><div class="eyebrow">CSE Tech Day · feasibility study · 16 October 2026</div><h1>{title}</h1>
<p class="lead">Recommendation: proceed as a small replication-and-extension project. There is a measurable local improvement over fixed-parameter QAOA, enough to justify a focused hardware experiment.</p>
<div class="callout"><strong>Completed:</strong> 800 toy instances, trained selectors, exact classical controls, Qiskit circuit verification, and synthetic noise tests. <strong>Planned:</strong> IBM hardware validation and a reusable public package. <strong>QPU usage so far: zero.</strong></div>
<h2>What the pilot actually established</h2><img alt="Bar chart showing fixed QAOA 2.95%, clustering 3.55%, and tree selection 3.61% in exact simulation" src="data:image/png;base64,{image_data}">
<p>On 200 fresh, distinct lattices, separately trained fixed parameters achieved <strong>2.949%</strong> average per-shot optimal-solution probability; clustering achieved <strong>3.553%</strong>; the frozen Extra Trees selector achieved <strong>3.615%</strong>. The tree-versus-fixed difference was <strong>+0.665 percentage points</strong> (exploratory paired 95% bootstrap interval <strong>+0.463 to +0.878</strong> points), a relative increase of about 22.6%.</p>
<p>The extra model complexity is not yet justified: the tree-versus-clustering difference on these fresh instances was only +0.061 percentage points, with an interval spanning zero (-0.026 to +0.156). Clustering is a credible lightweight primary method; the tree model is a useful comparison.</p>
<table><tr><th>Test</th><th>Observed result</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
<tr><td>Original 600-instance audit</td><td>542 distinct lattices; Babai solved 587/600; all 600 local boxes contained a global optimum</td><td>The encoding was adequate for this toy sample; no general coverage guarantee follows.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Certified BDD with nonzero error</td><td>205 original + 71 fresh instances; Babai solved every one</td><td>No evidence of a quantum decoding advantage on the promised BDD subset.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fresh 200-instance control</td><td>Babai solved 193/200; all local boxes covered an optimum</td><td>Classical decoding remains the useful solver at this scale.</td></tr>
<tr><td>24 instances, light synthetic noise</td><td>Fixed portfolio choice 2.454%; tree-selected choice 2.928%</td><td>A positive simulated sampling gain survives this noise model.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Same 24, moderate synthetic noise</td><td>Fixed portfolio choice 1.959%; tree-selected choice 2.283%</td><td>The effect is smaller under stronger noise. Physical-device transfer remains unknown.</td></tr>
<tr><td>96 Qiskit circuits</td><td>Ideal probabilities agree with the independent simulator to below 5 × 10⁻¹⁶, including routing</td><td>The encoding, circuit signs, qubit ordering, and routing permutation were cross-checked.</td></tr></table>
<p><small>Noise-test fixed parameters were selected per qubit count from the four-member portfolio. The stronger, separately trained per-size fixed baseline in the fresh noiseless comparison was added later as a control; it was not run through the noise test. Noise results are exact density-matrix probabilities, not measured shot counts.</small></p>
<div class="callout note"><strong>Keep the claim precise.</strong> This is an improvement in QAOA sampling relative to fixed-parameter QAOA. It is not a speedup over classical decoding, an attack on ML-KEM, or a measured improvement to TLS. The three/four-dimensional lattices and six/eight-qubit circuits are deliberately small and verifiable.</div>
<h2>The research question and modest contribution</h2>
<p><strong>Can public lattice features select pretrained QAOA parameters that improve optimal-sample probability on unseen toy lattice-decoding instances, and does that improvement survive a tightly budgeted IBM hardware experiment?</strong></p>
<p>The contribution is a reproducible empirical comparison on a cryptographically motivated benchmark family, with a small selection-method extension. Existing work supplies the lattice reduction, binary encoding, QAOA, angle pretraining, and general parameter-learning ideas. We do not need to claim a new quantum algorithm or a first-of-its-kind result.</p>
<p>PQC supplies the mathematical motivation; classical machine learning chooses settings; the quantum processor executes the QAOA circuits. Network security provides context because it motivates PQC adoption, but no network or TLS experiment is included in this scope.</p>
<h2>Prior work and how it changes the scope</h2><p>This is a targeted literature survey, not an exhaustive systematic review or a novelty certification. Searches covered LWE/BDD QAOA, fixed-angle lattice solvers, learned parameter transfer, noise-aware selection, and existing implementations. “Lattice” results about physical qubit layouts and spin systems were separated from cryptographic lattices.</p>
<table><tr><th>Source</th><th>Relevant prior result</th><th>Consequence for this project</th></tr>{table}</table>
<h2>Method and architecture</h2><div class="flow"><div><strong>1 · Verified instances</strong><br>Toy LWE-like data → q-ary lattice → exact reference answers and BDD labels.</div><div><strong>2 · Classical preprocessing</strong><br>LLL basis reduction → Babai estimate → bounded binary search → normalized QUBO.</div><div><strong>3 · Offline learning</strong><br>Train QAOA schedules; learn a geometry-based selection rule using training groups only.</div><div><strong>4 · Frozen inference</strong><br>Public features select one schedule; no secret, exact optimum, or test outcomes enter selection.</div><div><strong>5 · Quantum execution</strong><br>Local ideal/noisy simulators now; compiled IBM circuits and raw samples later.</div><div><strong>6 · Evaluation</strong><br>Exact verifier → baseline comparisons → uncertainty, gate counts, and QPU usage.</div></div>
<p><strong>Instance construction.</strong> Generate full-column-rank A over prime moduli 11, 17, or 23 with (sample dimension, secret dimension) equal to (3,1) or (4,2). Construct the column-basis lattice generated by [A | qI], reduce its Hermite-normal-form basis with LLL, and generate targets from modular linear combinations plus disclosed rounded-normal errors. This is a toy generator, not the ML-KEM distribution.</p>
<p><strong>Exact labels.</strong> Enumerate all qⁿ residue codewords and their nearest q-integer lifts to compute the global closest-vector distance. Compute the shortest nonzero lattice norm from centered residues and the q-unit vectors. Label a planted instance as certified BDD only when 4‖e‖² &lt; λ₁². A closest vector outside this promise need not recover the planted secret. Score all tied global minima as successes.</p>
<p><strong>Encoding.</strong> Starting from the public Babai coefficient vector z₀, encode each coefficient as zᵢ = z₀ᵢ − 2 + x₂ᵢ + 2x₂ᵢ₊₁. The objective is ‖Bz − t‖²; hence two bits per lattice dimension. Verify the QUBO/Ising energy against the direct squared norm for every bitstring. Normalize using the root-sum-square of nonconstant Ising coefficients, which does not require an exact solution. Measure global-optimum coverage separately so truncation cannot silently inflate success.</p>
<p><strong>Training.</strong> The original data were grouped by canonical lattice basis: 353 training, 129 validation, 118 test instances, with no lattice shared between partitions. Four p = 1 schedules were fitted locally: one on all training data and one per geometry cluster. Public features include normalized Gram geometry, conditioning, Babai residual, and Hamiltonian coefficient summaries. Validation selected Extra Trees from a small model comparison. A second evaluation froze the model and schedules before generating 200 new, distinct lattices. Every candidate uses the same depth and encoding.</p>
<p><strong>Controls.</strong> Keep uniform sampling, one fixed schedule per size, separately trained fixed schedules per size, cluster assignment, the tree selector, Babai, and exact enumeration. The best schedule chosen using test answers is only an unattainable portfolio ceiling. Include runtime and preprocessing cost if making any future efficiency comparison with classical methods. Keep raw quantum performance separate from any classical correction of measured samples.</p>
<h2>IBM experiment within the stated allowance</h2>
<p>Freeze the method and a fresh 24-instance hardware set before collecting outcomes. Use 12 six-qubit and 12 eight-qubit instances without choosing them by favorable performance. For each instance run the four pretrained schedules and a separately trained per-size fixed control: at most five settings. Execute 1,024 shots per setting in three randomized, interleaved rounds. This gives at most <strong>368,640 shots</strong>; identical settings can share samples and must be treated as paired observations.</p>
<p>IBM’s quick execution-only coefficient gives about <strong>129 seconds plus sub-job overhead</strong> for this design. This is a planning estimate, not a reservation or guarantee. First submit a small pilot, compile and estimate durations on the actual accessible backend, and check remaining allowance. Aim for at most 420 seconds of total reported usage, retaining a 180-second reserve. Apply conservative per-job caps and check cumulative usage before each subsequent submission. Batch time-to-live is not a cumulative execution cap.</p>
<p>The local mock-experiment calculation suggests 512 shots per circuit is weak for the smaller noise-model effect. At 3,072 total shots per circuit, 82.75% of 400 mock moderate-noise experiments produced a positive lower paired-bootstrap bound. This is conditional planning evidence for these 24 synthetic instances, not a prediction of IBM statistical power. Hardware may have different errors and instance variation.</p>
<p>Record backend identity, calibration timestamp, circuit layout, actual gate count/depth, compilation seed, raw counts, job IDs, and reported QPU seconds. The pilot line-topology circuits required 26–150 CX gates and depths of 38–138; actual IBM compilation may differ substantially. Use a fresh holdout for the final experiment because this pilot was exploratory.</p>
<h2>What to build next</h2>
<ol><li>Turn the verified generators, encodings, and classical controls into small reusable modules.</li><li>Freeze the clustering and tree-selection comparisons; repeat local tests across more seeds and one held-out modulus.</li><li>Compile a small hardware pilot and check whether the observed success is sufficiently above uniform sampling to justify the main run.</li><li>Run the budgeted IBM experiment, publish paired uncertainty and all failures, then package the raw evidence.</li></ol>
<p>A practical open-source deliverable would provide instance generation, exact verification for tiny cases, a circuit factory, baseline selectors, local simulation, a budget-checking IBM runner, and report generation. The current files are research prototypes; a public repository, license, installable package, and hardware runner have not yet been created. The value is reproducibility and honest comparison even if the more elaborate selector fails to beat clustering.</p>
<p><strong>Decision rules.</strong> Continue with this topic because the local fixed-versus-selected comparison is positive. Prefer the simpler selector if extra complexity has no repeatable gain. If hardware results are inconclusive, report the transfer limit instead of claiming a successful hardware improvement. Do not make calibration-aware selection central: on the moderate synthetic model the remaining gap between the current selector and the portfolio ceiling was only about 0.020 percentage points.</p>
<h2>Limitations and evidence status</h2>
<p>Only tiny dimensions, a few moduli, one QAOA layer, one initial exploratory split, and one fresh-data seed have been studied. The extra control comparisons were added after inspecting the first results, though fitting continued to use training data only. Confidence intervals are exploratory paired bootstrap intervals over lattice groups, conditional on the fitted model; they do not include uncertainty from retraining on new datasets. Synthetic noise omits real calibration variation, crosstalk, leakage, reset errors, drift, and many hardware details. No quantum hardware, network traffic, or deployed cryptographic implementation was tested.</p>
<h2>Submission abstract</h2><div class="abstract"><h3>{title}</h3>{''.join('<p>'+html.escape(p)+'</p>' for p in abstract.split(chr(10)+chr(10)))}</div>
<h2>Reproduce and inspect</h2><p>Run the three experiment files in order: <code>pilot/feasibility.py</code>, <code>pilot/hardware_readiness.py</code>, and <code>pilot/controls.py</code>. They make no cloud calls. Run <code>pilot/package_findings.py</code> to regenerate this brief and chart. Exact dependency versions are recorded in <code>pilot/requirements.txt</code>. JSON files in <code>outputs/</code> contain complete instance manifests, split IDs, trained schedules, probabilities, uncertainty estimates, and noise assumptions.</p>
</main></html>'''
